{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: review what actually happened in this project recently, and say what should change in the process or the skillfiles. Hugh asked for this on 2026-08-22; the ask is recorded in assert 2da0d79f.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORTH DOING, AND WHY IT USUALLY IS NOT. Retrospectives normally produce advice nobody can act on, because they are written from memory and memory keeps the stories rather than the frequencies. This project does not have that excuse. Every request, promise, report, verdict, refusal and merge is in the log with its timestamp, its author and its outcome. The ineffective acts are counted. The stale commitments are counted. The withdrawn ones are counted. This review should be arithmetic first and opinion second.\n\nTHE QUESTIONS THAT HAVE ANSWERS IN THE LOG. How much filed work was later retired and refiled, and for what reasons -- wrong basis, moved world, cancelled premise? How many acts were ruled ineffective, grouped by the fold's reason string, and which of those reasons appear again and again? How long does work sit between promise and report, and between report and merge? How often does a head get approved and then invalidated? How many requests were filed against a defect that had already been fixed? Which of those numbers is large enough to be worth a change, and which is folklore?\n\nThen the judgement. AGENTS.md and SKILL.md are the process. If a failure mode recurs and the skillfiles do not warn about it, that is the improvement. If they do warn and it recurs anyway, the warning is in the wrong place or the guard should be in code rather than prose. Say which, and be willing to conclude that a rule should be deleted rather than added -- the last simplification review found the ceremony too heavy, and a retrospective that only adds rules makes that worse.\n\nWHAT I WOULD NOT ACCEPT FROM ANOTHER ACTOR, so I should not produce it. A list of things that went wrong with no count attached. A recommendation that cannot be tested. A finding that is really one bad day. And no naming of actors as the cause: if an actor keeps making a mistake, the process let them.\n\nDeliver findings, not a head. Where a change is worth making, file a child request; where it needs agreement, file a proposal for hugh to ratify.","body":{"conditions":"Review the project's recent durable history and report what should change in the process or the skillfiles. (1) Measure from the log, not from recall: retired-and-refiled work with reasons; ineffective acts grouped by the fold's reason string; time from promise to report and report to merge; approvals invalidated by a changed head; requests filed against already-fixed defects; work that closed without a merge. State the window measured and the query used, so the numbers can be re-derived. (2) Rank the recurring failure modes by frequency and by what they cost, and say which are too rare to act on. (3) For each mode worth acting on, say whether AGENTS.md or SKILL.md already warns about it. If it does and the mode recurs, say whether the warning should move, become a code guard, or be dropped. (4) Include at least one recommendation to REMOVE process, and justify it; a retrospective that only adds rules makes the ceremony problem worse. (5) Attribute causes to the process, never to an actor. Deliver a report with the findings, file child requests for changes worth making, and file a proposal for anything that needs Hugh's ratification; this request is satisfied by the report, not by an implementation.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}